@* M-D3 owner ruling (U2), progress.md near-verbatim: "U2 xref expansion --
   down: +2 refs; up: -2; double-down: all; double-up: collapse to
   original." Extended by U6 to Catechism ("Catechism defaults to 2 shown
   + U2 mechanics") -- ONE component, parameterized, backing BOTH lists
   ("one component/behavior, parameterized -- never two implementations,"
   the brief's own words, the identical discipline PASSAGE-1/
   CAP-RECONCILE-1 already establish elsewhere in this popover platform).
   PassageList.razor uses this INTERNALLY for its own cross-references/
   THE SCRIPTURES/place est-dest reveal (REPLACES its retired binary
   Cap/_showAll all-or-nothing toggle); CatechismSeamSection uses it
   DIRECTLY (its own list is a plain button row, not a PassageList/
   PassageSourceUnit consumer, so it cannot go through that component --
   this one is deliberately shaped generically enough for either caller:
   a total count, a floor, a step, and a currently-shown count, nothing
   about WHAT is being shown).

   INTERACTION: a SINGLE click on the down arrow reveals Step MORE (never
   past Total); a single click on the up arrow collapses Step FEWER
   (never below Default -- "never below the default," the brief's own
   words).

   "DOUBLE-DOWN"/"DOUBLE-UP" -- M-D3 fix round 3 (R-D3, controller
   interpretation ruling, review Important-2): an EARLIER draft of this
   component read "double-down"/"double-up" as a native DOUBLE-CLICK
   gesture on the SAME single arrow, realized instead as Shift+click after
   a real, live-caught, reproducible bug: a genuine dblclick's two
   sub-clicks target ONE fixed screen coordinate, but this popover
   platform re-centers/repositions its OWN outer panel as content height
   changes, so the first sub-click's own Reveal() growing the list shifted
   the whole panel -- and this arrow's own on-screen position -- before the
   second sub-click landed, observed landing on a newly-revealed row
   instead and pushing THAT node. The controller's own re-reading of the
   owner's verbatim words ("double down arrow should expand all") is that
   "double [X] arrow" names a SECOND, always-paired ARROW BUTTON next to
   the single one -- not a double-CLICK gesture on the single arrow at
   all -- which sidesteps the whole hazard class by construction: TWO
   separate, stationary buttons never share one screen coordinate the way
   two sub-clicks of one dblclick do, so there is no repositioning race to
   have. Shift+click (the prior fix-round's own mitigation) is RETIRED
   here, not kept alongside these buttons -- one mechanism, not two
   competing ones (dead-code law) -- `MouseEventArgs.ShiftKey` is no longer
   read anywhere in this file. The double-arrow buttons render as a PAIR
   with their own single-step sibling (same conditional presence: the
   double-collapse pair appears exactly when Shown &gt; Default, the
   double-more pair exactly when Shown &lt; Total) -- "always visible"
   means "visible whenever there is somewhere further to jump to," not
   unconditionally.

   CONDITIONAL PRESENCE: renders nothing at all when Total &lt;= Default
   (nothing to reveal, same "no arrow at all under the cap" rule XREF-1
   always followed); the collapse pair is independently conditional on
   Shown &gt; Default, the more pair on Shown &lt; Total -- BOTH can render
   together in a genuine middle state (this component's own incremental
   model has one, unlike the retired all-or-nothing toggle, which never
   showed both at once).

   O2 (owner live-preview correction, 2026-08-23, "we have redundant
   buttons... Show the rest of this passage and Read the whole chapter...
   replace with just a down/double down arrow"): MiniReaderExpand.razor's
   OWN compact/whole-chapter toggle -- a genuinely BINARY state (Default=0/
   Total=1/Step=1 below, never a count of list entries) -- reuses this SAME
   component rather than growing a second implementation, but its two
   labels ("Read the whole chapter"/"+N more -- read the chapter" one way,
   "Show just this verse" the other) don't fit the generic "{N} more
   {RevealNoun}" template below at all (that template is built for counting
   LIST ENTRIES; a chapter isn't one). MoreLabel/CollapseLabel let a caller
   supply its own verbatim aria-label/title text instead, for exactly this
   "qualitatively different content, not just more of the same list" case
   -- optional, additive, every existing caller (PassageList's own list-
   level Cap reveal, CatechismSeamSection) leaves both null and keeps its
   byte-for-byte existing generated copy. The single- and double-arrow
   button on the SAME side share one override (both really do perform the
   identical jump in MiniReaderExpand's own binary case -- honestly
   identical text for an honestly identical action, not a bug).

   All four buttons stop click propagation (added for O2): MiniReaderExpand's
   own new usage nests inside PassageList.razor's clickable `.popover-
   passage-entry` (`@onclick="() => Explore(entry.Block)"`) the SAME way its
   own retired button always needed to guard against -- see that file's own
   comment. A no-op for every OTHER existing caller (PassageList's own
   list-level Cap reveal, CatechismSeamSection), neither of which nests
   inside a clickable ancestor, so this is safe to apply unconditionally
   here rather than growing a per-caller opt-in. *@
@if (Total > Default)
{
    <div class="popover-reveal-controls">
        @if (Shown > Default)
        {
            <button type="button" class="popover-reveal-arrow popover-reveal-collapse-all" data-testid="@($"{CollapseTestId}-all")"
                    aria-label="@(CollapseLabel ?? $"Collapse all {RevealNoun} to the default view")"
                    title="@(CollapseLabel ?? $"Collapse to the default view ({Default} {RevealNoun})")"
                    @onclick="OnCollapseAllClick" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">&#9652;&#9652;</button>
            <button type="button" class="popover-reveal-arrow popover-reveal-collapse" data-testid="@CollapseTestId"
                    aria-label="@(CollapseLabel ?? $"Show fewer {RevealNoun}")"
                    title="@(CollapseLabel ?? $"Show {Math.Min(Step, Shown - Default)} fewer {RevealNoun}")"
                    @onclick="OnCollapseClick" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">&#9652;</button>
        }
        else
        {
            @* Same-sized, empty placeholders -- NOT the buttons, kept
               entirely out of the DOM when absent (`.toHaveCount(0)` stays
               a true "does not exist" check for CollapseTestId/its own
               `-all` sibling, the same conditional-presence guarantee
               every other reveal arrow in this app already gives). Their
               ONLY job is to hold the collapse pair's own slot at a fixed
               width regardless of whether the real buttons are present --
               see the more pair's own slots, .popover-reveal-slot, for why
               this matters (the SAME position-stability reasoning the
               dblclick-rejection story above already established, now
               protecting two stationary buttons instead of one moving
               target -- a different failure mode, the identical fix). *@
            <span class="popover-reveal-slot"></span>
            <span class="popover-reveal-slot"></span>
        }
        @if (Shown < Total)
        {
            <button type="button" class="popover-reveal-arrow popover-reveal-more" data-testid="@MoreTestId"
                    aria-label="@(MoreLabel ?? $"Show {Math.Min(Step, Total - Shown)} more {RevealNoun}")"
                    title="@(MoreLabel ?? $"Show {Math.Min(Step, Total - Shown)} more {RevealNoun}")"
                    @onclick="OnMoreClick" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">&#9662;</button>
            <button type="button" class="popover-reveal-arrow popover-reveal-more-all" data-testid="@($"{MoreTestId}-all")"
                    aria-label="@(MoreLabel ?? $"Show all {Total} {RevealNoun}")"
                    title="@(MoreLabel ?? $"Show all {Total} {RevealNoun}")"
                    @onclick="OnMoreAllClick" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">&#9662;&#9662;</button>
        }
    </div>
}

@code {
    // The list's own true total size -- never re-derived from Shown/Step,
    // always the caller's own ground truth (PassageList's own _blocks.Count,
    // CatechismSeamSection's own items.Count).
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Total { get; set; }

    // The floor collapsing never crosses -- the SAME number the list's own
    // initial render already shows (PassageList's existing Cap parameter,
    // CatechismSeamSection's own new 2, F2/CAP-RECONCILE-1's 2-vs-3 xrefs
    // rule) -- "never below the default," the owner's own words.
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Default { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public int Step { get; set; } = 2;

    // Two-way bound -- the caller owns the actual state (its own _shown
    // field), this component only ever proposes the next value via
    // ShownChanged, never mutates anything itself (a stateless control,
    // matching PassageList.razor's/MiniReaderExpand.razor's own established
    // "per-render state lives in the CALLER, not silently duplicated in a
    // shared child" discipline).
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public int Shown { get; set; }
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public EventCallback<int> ShownChanged { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string MoreTestId { get; set; } = "reveal-more";
    [Parameter] public string CollapseTestId { get; set; } = "reveal-collapse";
    [Parameter] public string RevealNoun { get; set; } = "entries";

    // O2: verbatim aria-label/title override for the more/more-all (resp.
    // collapse/collapse-all) pair, for a caller whose reveal isn't "N more
    // list entries" -- see this file's own header comment. Null (every
    // pre-existing caller) keeps the generated "{N} more {RevealNoun}" copy
    // exactly as before.
    [Parameter] public string? MoreLabel { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public string? CollapseLabel { get; set; }

    private Task OnMoreClick() => ShownChanged.InvokeAsync(Math.Min(Shown + Step, Total));

    private Task OnCollapseClick() => ShownChanged.InvokeAsync(Math.Max(Shown - Step, Default));

    private Task OnMoreAllClick() => ShownChanged.InvokeAsync(Total);

    private Task OnCollapseAllClick() => ShownChanged.InvokeAsync(Default);
}
